WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:05.569
An aurora shimmers the night sky.

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:12.500
And for a moment, a startling, seemingly alien
realm becomes illuminated.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.941
This is the world of Serina… and despite
appearances, these are not aliens, but the

00:00:17.941 --> 00:00:21.140
descendants of birds… and fish.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:27.810
We’ve been following this saga for several
videos, watching as, over the past two hundred

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:33.540
and eighty million years, an initial group
of finches and other creatures left on a distant

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:38.030
moon have evolved into a dizzying variety
of forms.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:43.260
When we last checked in, this world had been
flung into cataclysm, and the intelligent

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.180
Sea Stewards had been transported away to
another realm.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:53.230
Now, Serina is beginning to recover from the
loss of its top species — although the greatest

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:57.690
dangers and most extortionary lifeforms still
lie ahead.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:03.700
So, let’s return to this world of birds,
and remember to support creator Dylan Bajda

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.250
on Patreon using the links below.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.580
Our journey begins with the rising sun.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:18.229
Here in the Northern Soglands, the morning
rays illuminate a varied tapestry of life.

00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:23.140
Thorngrazers feed upon the sodden plant matter
and unleash their bellowing calls.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.420
A predatory Drakevulture scours for prey from
above.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.479
The towering Watchtower Wumpos loom over the
entire scene.

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:38.479
These species represent some of the lineages
that will come to prominence in this new era.

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:44.729
We are now in The Hothouse Age, and the lands
of Serina have become splintered by the highest-ever

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:46.070
sea level.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:51.219
Though volcanism is on the decline and the
end of this world looms ever closer — an

00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:57.640
atmosphere heavy in carbon-dioxide means Serina
has never been so warm… or so conducive

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.480
to radical experimentation.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.950
On the inland floodplains, a monstrous descendant
of the Burrowing Burdle attempts to bring

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:10.819
down a smaller Hoglump, while a Foxtrotter
watches from the branches.

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:17.610
All three species have had to radically change
their behavior in a short period to survive.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:22.599
On the ever-shifting coast, adaptability is
even more essential.

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:28.010
This tentacled Shorescrounger and their chicks
are just humble scavengers at present — but

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.739
there’s no telling what the future will
bring.

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:35.680
From the water emerges a Sandlubber — a
kind of semi-terrestrial snail.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.580
To the Shorescroungers, it looks like breakfast.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:44.880
In the open sea, other snail-descendants are
enjoying a buffet of their own.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.270
The snaggletoothed Calacarna have become huge
apex predators, and their preferred prey — the

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:55.019
Escardines — are also a type of marine snail.

00:02:55.019 --> 00:03:00.970
The seas belong to the invertebrates now,
and as the eons pass, the gastropods’ grip

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:07.121
upon the marine ecosystem will only tighten
as larger and larger species arise in the

00:03:07.121 --> 00:03:09.170
post-ice-age waters.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:14.769
Some, like the Spikerays, will proliferate
under the murky surface of freshwater rivers

00:03:14.769 --> 00:03:22.580
in astonishingly high numbers — their body-plans
converging upon that of modern ray species.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:26.280
Another dawn comes to this brave new world.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.530
On the northern coast of Serinaustra, the
body of a titanic Shimmershiner — currently

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:35.909
the largest marine snail species — washes
up on the sands.

00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:40.090
All manner of carnivores quickly descend upon
this rare feast.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.400
There’s no time to take turns.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:49.370
Eventually, night falls, and a very peculiar
creature emerges to assess what remains of

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:50.370
the carcass.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:56.069
It’s a Nightcreeper — another Burrowing
Burdle descendant that’s strictly nocturnal.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:04:00.849
The early bird might get the biggest portion,
but the Nightcreeper has carved out a niche

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:05.220
for itself snatching up midnight leftovers.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.720
Of course, not everyone has to stress about
sticking to the shadows.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:15.390
The omnivorous Skoblin — descendants of
the otter-like skuorc birds — have arrived

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:18.230
upon a rather imposing body plan.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:22.650
The future of this lineage will be very bright
indeed.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:28.740
More familiar dynasties flourish in one of
the last relatively dry biomes on Serina — the

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:29.740
Uplands.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:35.550
Here, members of the Battering Helmethead
Thorngrazer species clash over territory close

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.180
to a herd of Jumpo Wumpos.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:43.620
A relative of the Watchtower Wumpos, they’re
far from the strangest trunked bird species

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:45.090
in this era.

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:50.440
In the southern jungles, for example, the
speedy Lumpelopes race through the underbrush,

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:54.940
their facial flanges displaying bright colors
to attract mates.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:05:00.430
In the polar basin, the peculiar Shucklumps
have turned their trunks into a means of prying

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.150
open shellfish for an easy meal.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:08.900
The Shucklumps appear nonthreatening, but
the Laship Carnackles — an emergent carnivorous

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.660
trunk bird, appear anything but.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:19.370
And in this case, appearances aren’t deceiving:
the Lashlips are ruthlessly effective hunters.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:24.970
The Hothouse age can be ruthless, but it can
also be a stage for unexpectedly touching

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:25.970
moments.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:31.840
Here, a mother Bloblump — the most rotund
of the trunk birds — has taken in a rather

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:37.090
confused Foxtrotter, who has gained the scent
of her young after spending time in their

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:38.090
vicinity.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:43.240
Though its roaming instincts will eventually
cause this Foxtrotter to wander on, it is

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:47.400
nice to be part of a family — if just for
a day.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:52.870
Not long ago, Serina was a haven for this
sort of cross-species cooperation.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:58.470
But the most intelligent and altruistic species
have all since vanished — displaced to another

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:03.790
world or rendered extinct by the calamity
that capped off the previous era.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:10.650
Though deeply tragic, this loss has left behind
an intriguing intelligence vacuum.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:16.050
Back on the coast, the Shorescroungers have
begun to diversify, with some new offshoots

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.199
becoming increasingly social.

00:06:18.199 --> 00:06:21.780
It’s a development to keep an eye on…

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:27.900
As the Houthouse age wears on, new forest
ecosystems offer new ways of life.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:33.650
Here, a Gravedigger subspecies sinks its teeth
into a hapless Thorngrazer.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:38.120
You wouldn’t know it by glancing at the
predator, but its distant relatives — the

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:43.770
Thalassic Gravediggers, were once one of the
most intelligent species on Serina.

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:49.461
Elsewhere in the forest, however, a different
subspecies is following more closely in the

00:06:49.461 --> 00:06:52.250
Thalassic Gravedigger’s footsteps.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:57.520
This is a Spirepryer, a primitive tool user
who feeds on Honey Ants.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:04.080
They’re a reasonably brainy bird: although
not half as clever as their Thalassic relatives.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.590
Another species with a remarkable ancestry
is the Skreehonk.

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:14.330
These long-necked herbivores are members of
the Giraffowl — an emerging clade that,

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:19.460
like earth giraffes, browse on vegetation
that most grazers simply can’t reach.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.610
But where did such an unusual clade emerge
from?

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:27.830
They’re actually the distant descendants
of the Seraphs, who once reigned supreme in

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:29.310
Serina’s skies.

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:35.759
One small surviving lineage, the Fallen Angels,
found a niche scavenging on the ground, becoming

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:39.330
increasingly well-adapted for terrestrial
locomotion.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:45.620
From this initial Giraffowl body plan, a wide
variety of unusual offshoots have emerged.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:51.740
And over the next several million years, this
group will only become stranger.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:57.940
But for now, the Giraffefowl have competition
in the Hothouse forests from Spiral Sirenhorns

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:03.460
— a type of Thorngrazer with an elaborate
nasal structure that produces eerie musical

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:05.270
calls.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:10.909
These pack animals have reached impressive
sizes, converging on a niche similar to modern

00:08:10.909 --> 00:08:12.080
bison.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:17.630
Yet both the Sirenhorns and the Giraffefowl
need to keep an eye on a new upstart.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:23.470
Soggoblers are huge, aggressive creatures
related to the Skoblins that seem poised to

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:27.500
follow in the titanic footsteps of Earth sauropods.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:33.270
And while the Soggoblers are herbivores, the
related Skogres have a taste for meat as well

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:34.270
as veggies.

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:39.700
At three and a half meters, or twelve feet
tall, the only thing more formidable than

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:42.899
their monstrous size is their monstrous appetite.

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:49.050
A group of Skogre can strip a small island
of vegetation in just a few weeks, before

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:52.350
crossing the water to do the same thing to
another landmass.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.910
One might assume that nothing could rival
their destructive power…

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:02.550
…But in the southern bogs, an even more
ravenous force is waiting.

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:08.019
This trunked bird… is about to fall prey
to the insatiable Bog Shogoth.

00:09:08.019 --> 00:09:15.540
A superorganism made up of thousands of cooperating
ants, Bog Shogoths are the ultimate scavengers.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:21.300
Though slightly less aggressive than their
ice age ancestors, the Sea Shogoths, the fact

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:26.720
such a deadly lineage has endured into the
Hothouse era could spell trouble for the age

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:27.720
to come.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:31.010
It’s a good time to be up in the branches.

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:36.459
The forest canopy is now home to not just
bird descendants, but snail descendants as

00:09:36.459 --> 00:09:37.459
well.

00:09:37.459 --> 00:09:43.210
The peculiar Tree Gups are derived from marine
snails that became amphibious during the start

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:48.400
of the Hothouse, and are now well adapted
for a wooded environment.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:55.170
Once, the woods of Serina were tended to by
the Antlears — one of the first intelligent

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:59.500
tool users who tragically faded from history
long ago.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:04.850
Though their allies, the Gravediggers, preserved
their memory for a time, the Gravediggers

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:08.160
have now also disappeared from Serina’s
surface.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:15.040
But while these Antlears are long extinct,
one lone relative, the Surivears, have endured

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.810
into the middle Hothouse.

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:22.370
And as a changing climate turns marshland
to savannah, the Antlears have an incredible

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:25.800
opportunity to adapt...

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:30.350
We now return to the image of the aurora-lit
swampland.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:35.750
This scene takes place in Serina’s warm
southern pole, where for several months, the

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:37.070
sun does not rise.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:39.350
And in the darkness…

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:43.029
evolution has sired creatures never-before-seen.

00:10:43.029 --> 00:10:48.800
Shimmering bioluminescence — a rare sight
in terrestrial organisms, lights up sections

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:50.610
of the polar forest.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:56.170
On the forest floor, a Digdag — another
Burrowing Bundle descendant scrounges for

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:57.290
a meal.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:11:02.800
Close to this badger-sized lifeform is a fan
club of smaller fliers, who are hopeful the

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.100
Digdag will uncover something they can steal.

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:12.220
But all smaller lifeforms need to tread carefully,
for the uncanny Spook hides in the shroud

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:13.220
of night.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:18.670
A member of the batlike lineage of tribbats,
the Spook’s large ears form a dish-like

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:23.190
structure that allows them to hone in on the
slightest sound.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:27.829
Yet Spooks are not the canniest hunters in
the Longdark Swamp.

00:11:27.829 --> 00:11:33.060
Something very special has emerged in this
darkness: a new tool user.

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:38.700
Squabgoblins are relatives of Shorescroungers
that have put their tentacles to work crafting

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:40.450
rudimentary spears.

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:46.120
This, combined with their ability to hunt
in packs, have made these tiny terrors formidable

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:47.120
indeed.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.459
Though the Squabgoblins currently lack the
true spark of sapience, there is enough kindling

00:11:52.459 --> 00:11:58.410
here to one day, hopefully, reignite the lost
fire of advanced intelligence…

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:04.790
And observing the Squabgoblins is another
bright-eyed bird species.

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:09.820
Bogglebirds are clever omnivores who have
learned to act as watchdogs for the Squabgoblins

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:11.550
in exchange for food.

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:16.529
Perhaps they, too, will carry the torch of
intelligence onwards…

00:12:16.529 --> 00:12:20.970
For now, day is breaking at last after many
months of darkness.

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:27.420
And with the light come the Dawnwalkers — large
bipedal herbivores that hibernate during the

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:32.420
winter, only to reawaken when the sun and
the greenery returns.

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:35.930
And they aren’t the only giants who return
with the sun.

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:42.350
Huge Giraffefowl called Crowncrested Skybreakers
descend upon the forest, coming home following

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:46.190
their long migration north for the duration
of the winter.

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:52.230
During the height of summer, the southern
swamp becomes almost unrecognizable as biodiversity

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:53.339
increases.

00:12:53.339 --> 00:13:00.880
One rather unassuming species, the aquatic
Rivercarver, acts as steward of this biodiversity.

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:07.139
Like earth beavers, Rivercarvers are ecosystem
engineers, damming and shifting the flow of

00:13:07.139 --> 00:13:11.910
freshwater to create an optimal environment
in which to raise their young.

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:17.600
A parent must do what they can — for it’s
a dangerous world out there.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:23.420
Some of the largest birds ever seen on Serina
now prowl the northern continent.

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:28.760
Descendants of the Soggobblers, the Cygnosaurs
might technically be herbivores, but they’re

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:31.100
hardly strict vegetarians.

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:36.019
These aggressive creatures will happily supplement
their diet with small animal life should the

00:13:36.019 --> 00:13:39.490
need arise — including chicks of their own
species.

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:45.389
Though this tree will keep these hatchings
safe, with the rise of the Cygnosaurs, survival

00:13:45.389 --> 00:13:48.209
on Serina now is far more challenging.

00:13:48.209 --> 00:13:51.100
It’s a good time to have allies.

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:56.829
The Superb Squaboon are relatives of the Longdark
Squabgoblins that have learned to cooperate

00:13:56.829 --> 00:13:59.620
in packs and craft simple tools.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:03.050
And they’re not the only crafty tentacle-bird
on the block.

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:08.470
The unusual Boomer Squaboon also operates
in clans, and have learned to build traps

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:13.649
to catch fish — although the primary function
of their strange tentacles is to amplify their

00:14:13.649 --> 00:14:16.149
mating calls like a megaphone.

00:14:16.149 --> 00:14:21.670
Next to these species, the Bloodbreasted Squaboon
doesn’t seem to have much going for it beyond

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:23.190
an unusual hue.

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:28.500
Yet out of all the Squaboons, they are the
ones with the grandest destiny…

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:32.880
For now, the end of the Hothouse age is looming
ever closer.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:38.620
Yet before this era comes to a close, a final
specular environment will emerge…

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:44.130
We are now two-hundred and ninety-million
years into the Serina experiment.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:49.460
And though tectonic shifts have slowed to
a crawl, new mountains rise against the distant

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:50.490
sky.

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:56.839
These Sky Islands are actually vast terrestrial
reefs formed from layers of Spire Trees — and

00:14:56.839 --> 00:15:01.899
they serve as a stage for remarkable new evolutionary
dramas.

00:15:01.899 --> 00:15:05.660
Gliding between the cliffs is a Superb Sky
Serpent.

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:12.130
A type of limbless Skuorc, these hunters can
soar for short distances by catching air underneath

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:16.880
their parachute-like bodies, much like Draco
lizards here on Earth.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:21.380
Other snake-like predators have a different
tactic for getting the drop on prey.

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:26.420
Cliff Strikeworms lurk in the crevices of
the Sky Islands, lashing out with their curved

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:31.240
mandibles to ensnare anything unlucky enough
to be caught unaware.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:37.090
But the crags of the Sky islands don’t just
hide predators — they also conceal prey.

00:15:37.090 --> 00:15:43.380
Here, an herbivorous Starrybara takes shelter
from the long posterior limb of a Skyheel.

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:48.350
A bite from the Starrybara’s beak might
be enough to dissuade the Skyheel from digging

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:55.730
deeper into its domain — although in the
game of life, these two species are well matched…

00:15:55.730 --> 00:16:01.420
The Sky Islands are one of many new fields
of competition in the late Hothouse.

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:06.829
In the arctic, a northern equivalent to the
Longdark Swamp has emerged in the form of

00:16:06.829 --> 00:16:08.560
the Night Forest.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:13.840
This massive vertical ecosystem is home to
trees so tall that the branches feel like

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:17.480
an entirely separate world from the forest
floor.

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:23.130
Over one hundred meters, or three hundred
and fifty feet off the ground, a Red Rasp

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.700
rests in the branches.

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:30.670
Though their hooked tongue appears fearsome,
the Rasp primarily feeds on insect larva that

00:16:30.670 --> 00:16:32.949
they dig from the bark of trees.

00:16:32.949 --> 00:16:40.000
A world away on the forest floor, the baffling
Prismuffalope also enjoys the occasional insect

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:41.000
snack.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:45.630
A descendant of the Battering Helmetheads,
Prismuffalopes sport highly unusual heads

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:49.779
crests both to impress mates, and scare off
rivals.

00:16:49.779 --> 00:16:56.300
At the very edge of the Nightforest, a tiny
equine-like relative Prismuffalope, the Equinox,

00:16:56.300 --> 00:17:01.399
is about to be preyed upon by the Ruffled
Rasp — a relative of the Red Rasp.

00:17:01.399 --> 00:17:09.689
It’s a tricky task for such a small predator,
but these unusual times call for risky behavior.

00:17:09.689 --> 00:17:13.280
For the late hothouse is an era of uncertainty.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:18.189
In the oceans, life is increasingly pushed
to the coast as the global climate wreaks

00:17:18.189 --> 00:17:20.890
havoc on deep water environments.

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:27.660
The result is a shallow water ecosystem of
extreme competition— and extreme lifeforms.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:32.960
One of the most unusual are the Wandering
Whelicans — new arrivals on the aquatic

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:38.590
scene that, despite appearances, are actually
the descendants of tentacled Shorescroungers

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:40.300
that took to the sea.

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:45.520
Like earth whales, Whelicans have evolved
to filter feed, and have radiated out into

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:51.520
a number of unusual forms — including the
Striker Whelican, whose outer two tentacles

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:54.549
have elongated into highly effective fish-catchers.

00:17:54.549 --> 00:18:00.770
Yet as predators, the Whelican’s pale in
comparison to the colossal Kraviathans — the

00:18:00.770 --> 00:18:04.730
single largest hunters ever to exist on Serina.

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:10.761
Exceeding fifty-two feet, or sixteen meters
in length and weighing up to forty tons, these

00:18:10.761 --> 00:18:16.150
giants are, amazingly, yet another descendant
of marine slugs.

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:22.130
Though the end of this world creeps ever-closer,
Serina has entered an age of giants — where

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:26.929
predator and prey clash like never before.

00:18:26.929 --> 00:18:32.789
In the southern pole, a giant Dawnwalker tries
to frighten off a sleek Fanguar.

00:18:32.789 --> 00:18:38.059
Fanguars are capable hunters, but this one
isn’t fully grown yet, and might be in over

00:18:38.059 --> 00:18:41.010
its head given the size of its quarry.

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:47.169
In the Sky Islands, a far smaller drama unfolds
between a Painted Jackalope and a long-toothed

00:18:47.169 --> 00:18:48.169
Goatsucker.

00:18:48.169 --> 00:18:53.610
Neither species weighs more than two kilograms,
or four pounds — but their will to survive

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:57.400
is no less fierce than the largest of Serina’s
animals.

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:03.539
And speaking of… on the northern continent,
certain Cygnosaurs are now among the largest

00:19:03.539 --> 00:19:05.789
animals ever to walk on Serina.

00:19:05.789 --> 00:19:10.220
But with exceptional prey comes exceptional
predators.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:15.460
Subjugators are a relatively new guild of
megafaunal bipedal tribbetheres large enough

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:18.640
to pose a threat to juvenile Cygnosaurs.

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:24.179
Skirmishes between these two groups are often
awe-inspiring — a clash of titans not seen

00:19:24.179 --> 00:19:27.400
since the Mesozoic of Earth.

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:33.920
Relatives of the Cygnosaurs, the Regal Skulossi,
also seem like something out of the age of

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:34.920
dinosaurs.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:39.049
These huge Skuorcs might appear too large
to be under threat of predation… but the

00:19:39.049 --> 00:19:42.210
late Hothouse is a time of monsters.

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:48.350
The Imperious River Dragon is a leviathan
large enough to drag Skulossi into the murky

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:49.350
depths.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:54.670
When pondering such a beast, it’s easy to
think of nature as a constant escalation of

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:56.250
violence.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:58.700
But nature has a silly side as well.

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:03.789
Elsewhere on the northern continent, Bowerbuck
are a type of Giraffowl that work hard to

00:20:03.789 --> 00:20:08.650
construct colorful towers of vegetation to
impress potential mates.

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:14.730
This sort of behavior can attract predators
as well — but such are the perils of love.

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:17.030
Some love stories are wholly unique.

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:22.169
Here, a male Flutterfox is trying to court
a large female Moonbeast.

00:20:22.169 --> 00:20:27.539
These two species aren’t closely related,
but the Flutterfox doesn’t seem deterred.

00:20:27.539 --> 00:20:32.419
It’s a small reminder that the engine of
nature will create sights one might never

00:20:32.419 --> 00:20:34.710
expect.

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:39.360
Wandering Spiderweeds are a type of carnivorous
plant that have learned to ‘move’ through

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:41.799
a cycle of continuous leaf replacement.

00:20:41.799 --> 00:20:47.240
They aren’t particularly quick — only
able to move around two meters, or six feet

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:52.400
per year — but they’re an intriguing species
that has quietly evolved in the background

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:53.809
of Serina.

00:20:53.809 --> 00:20:59.210
And on the subject of species that have evolved
in the background, Antlears have now diversified

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:02.130
into numerous successful forms.

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:07.510
Buldozears are large omnivores with antlers
powerful enough to stun both predators, and

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:09.039
mating rivals.

00:21:09.039 --> 00:21:13.409
Other Bulldozears have evolved hooked antlers
designed for quick slashes.

00:21:13.409 --> 00:21:18.670
Though none of these Antlears have continued
to carry the torch of intelligence their Woodcrafter

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:24.830
cousins once bore, they are nonetheless capable
lifeforms in their own right.

00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:29.970
Considering Serina’s current biodiversity,
it is difficult to imagine that the end is

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:30.970
near.

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:36.120
The northern Polar Basin, for example, has
never been as rich with life as it is two

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:38.940
hundred and ninety million years post establishment.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:43.880
But there are warning signs on the horizon
that cannot be ignored.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:49.850
As volcanism continues to decline, Serina’s
magnetic field weakens — and with it, the

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:56.179
atmosphere has started to thin, allowing for
increased harmful radiation to reach the planet’s

00:21:56.179 --> 00:21:57.179
surface.

00:21:57.179 --> 00:22:00.150
The final chapter has come at last.

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:05.470
As a mass extinction looms on the surface,
it is time we briefly turn our attention to

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:07.500
a hidden world below.

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:12.840
For underneath the exterior of Serina is a
network of chambers home to long forgotten

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:16.530
lineages that have had eons to adapt to the
dark.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:22.669
Very little of the life within these caverns
is currently known, but as the surface declines,

00:22:22.669 --> 00:22:26.410
an underground world might rise…

00:22:26.410 --> 00:22:30.190
Back on the surface, somewhere in the Longdark
Swamp, a group of Squabgoblins and Bogglebirds

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:31.840
watch the setting sun.

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:37.510
A cold snap has frozen the marsh solid, and
though this dip in temperature will not last,

00:22:37.510 --> 00:22:40.549
it is a sign of more permanent changes to
come.

00:22:40.549 --> 00:22:46.670
Will Serina’s burgeoning intelligent life
be able to adapt in time?

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:51.860
Jumping forward a brief five million years
beyond the end of the Hothouse age, a family

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:56.500
of Flutterfoxes watch a forest fire engulf
their environment.

00:22:56.500 --> 00:23:02.120
Rain no longer extinguishes these brushfires
as the atmosphere continues to weaken.

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:04.809
The end is very close indeed.

00:23:04.809 --> 00:23:09.529
But here in Serina’s twilight hours, Sapience
has returned at last.

00:23:09.529 --> 00:23:15.169
The Bloodbreasted Squaboon of ages past have
given rise to the Sylvanspark — a highly

00:23:15.169 --> 00:23:18.809
social species that is beginning to craft
complex tools.

00:23:18.809 --> 00:23:21.720
And they’re not the only tool-using tentacle
bird.

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:27.590
They share this world with the Slaughtersprinters
— descendants of Squabgoblins who have taken

00:23:27.590 --> 00:23:31.490
to the plains following the collapse of the
Longdark Swamp ecosystem.

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:37.169
And their Bogglebird allies have achieved
advanced intelligence as well, becoming the

00:23:37.169 --> 00:23:39.960
artistically inclined Whisperwings.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:45.120
All three species have arisen in close proximity
to each other — and their futures shall

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:48.370
be deeply intertwined.

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:50.640
But is it too little too late?

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:55.559
With the age of giants coming to a crashing
close and global temperatures dropping, it

00:23:55.559 --> 00:23:59.090
is unclear what hope these new sophonts have.

00:23:59.090 --> 00:24:04.830
Serina has always been a story of hope and
tragedy, of joy and sorrow.

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:10.120
Whatever the future holds, life is always
full of surprises…

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:13.890
The final chapter of the End Ultimocene is
still to come.

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:16.280
But that’s all I can give away for now.

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:21.250
If you enjoy this series as much as I do,
once again there are links to Dylan Bajda’s

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:26.240
Patreon and the Patreon of guest artist TrollMan
in the description.

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:29.660
And by the way, as always, thank you for watching.

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:34.680
If you enjoyed this video and want to support
the channel, consider liking, subscribing,

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:39.920
and hitting the notification icon to stay
up to date on all things curious.

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:41.179
See you in the next video.

